{"id":"compact-family","version":1,"when":"Small family homes where space and daily convenience matter.","combinations":"Combine kitchen and dining; consider laundry, storage and a modest covered outdoor room before optional amenities.","placement":"Keep three bedrooms private from entry and living; connect kitchen to dining and utility access with short circulation.","principles":"Use efficient shared boundaries and useful daylight; do not sacrifice bedrooms for luxury extras.","capabilities":"Rooms need explicit walls, openings and access; no automatic floor-plan or circulation solver."}
{"id":"facade-materials","version":1,"when":"A residence needing coherent enclosure and material expression.","combinations":"Coordinate a small material palette, roof edges, recessed entrances and shaded openings.","placement":"Relate facade rhythm to rooms and privacy; carry architectural intent around side and rear elevations.","principles":"Adapt material and roof recommendations to climate and style instead of prescribing a fixed house template.","capabilities":"Existing ivory, limestone, timber, bronze and sage; flat, gable and hip roofs; curved roof only via approved open pavilion."}
{"id":"garage-arrival","version":1,"when":"Vehicle access is requested or a residence needs an arrival strategy.","combinations":"Combine sheltered pedestrian entry, storage and discreet service access.","placement":"Connect parking to entrance and utility spaces without making garage doors the principal facade; protect walking routes.","principles":"Scale parking to the brief and available site, never infer a large car collection from price alone.","capabilities":"Composed volumes, plates and doors supported; no automatic vehicle turning simulation or garage-door mechanism."}

